WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.440
Martin Scorsese's Netflix original film The
Irishman is really getting dragged online

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.820
- so the official Twitter account Netflix
Film decided to do a little good-natured dragging

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:10.320
back.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:11.520
Hey, it's only fair.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.720
The account responded to a tweet - in which
it wasn't even tagged - that made a joke about

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:20.189
the best way to watch The Irishman, a film
that was produced for Netflix but received

00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:21.620
a limited theatrical run.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.180
In a recent interview with film critic Peter
Travers, the legendary director suggested

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:30.120
that The Irishman should definitely be viewed
on the biggest screen possible.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:34.580
"I would suggest, if you ever want to see
one of my pictures, or most films - please,

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:38.360
please don't look at it on a phone, please."

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.520
Scorsese goes on to say,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.680
"An iPad, a big iPad, maybe."

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.200
Well, you probably know where this is going.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.489
In response to that remark, several cheeky
troublemakers took to Twitter, posting pictures

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:53.649
of themselves watching The Irishman on every
diminutive device known to man.

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:58.249
For example, one tweet featured a viewer watching
Scorsese's film on an Apple Watch, along with

00:00:58.249 --> 00:00:58.780
the caption:

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.300
"#NowWatching The Irishman the way Scorsese
intended"

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:07.300
Another joker - who clearly possesses some
superior Photoshop skills - seemed to be watching

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.370
the film on old-school Nintendo Game Boy.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:11.370
The caption read:

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.080
"He said I could watch on this."

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.560
But the comment that drew the attention of
Netflix Film came from Comic Book Debate lead

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.120
writer Donnia Harrington, who cheekily claimed:

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:25.100
"[I] debated watching The Irishman on my phone
for this four-hour bus ride but I feel like

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.890
Scorsese will magically appear and beat my
ass if I do."

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:33.030
The Netflix account suggested that Harrington
was half right, replying with:

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:37.850
"He absolutely WILL appear but instead of
a beating he will simply sit across from you

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:42.660
for the entire film and twinkle his eyes in
a grandfatherly way that says 'it's ok my

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:47.060
child, formats and presentation matter, but
your experience of art is valid no matter

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.020
how you receive it.'"

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.020
Pretty funny, sure... but it doesn't really
sound like something Scorsese would say, does

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:53.520
it?

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.400
Perhaps that's because, in recent months,
the legendary filmmaker has offered up an

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:02.660
awful lot of opinions about mainstream film
- and they haven't exactly been well-received.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:07.260
The Great Scorsese Blowback of 2019 began
with three little words, uttered during an

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.850
interview with Empire magazine in early October:

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.520
"That's not cinema."

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:16.480
The director was referring to superhero films
in general, and the movies of the Marvel Cinematic

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:17.930
Universe in particular.

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:22.470
Needless to say, many fans of the MCU franchise
- along with some actors and directors who

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.040
worked on the films - took issue with that
statement.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:30.440
Scorsese's subsequent attempts to "clarify"
his remark didn't go particularly well, either.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:34.890
As The Hollywood Reporter noted, he effectively
doubled down a couple weeks after his Empire

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.380
magazine interview, saying,

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.720
"Theaters have become amusement parks.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.100
That is all fine and good but don't invade
everything else in that sense.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.520
[...] It's not my kind of thing, it simply
is not.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.100
It's creating another kind of audience that
thinks cinema is that."

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:54.060
In response to the mounting criticism over
these comments, Scorsese penned an editorial

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.460
for The New York Times in early November entitled,

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:58.700
"I said Marvel movies aren't cinema.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:02:59.620
Let me explain."

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.190
The lengthy piece basically fleshes out his
original statement.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:04.200
At one point, he writes,

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.920
"Many of the elements that define cinema as
I know it are there in Marvel pictures.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.640
What’s not there is revelation, mystery
or genuine emotional danger.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:14.920
Nothing is at risk."

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:20.010
Once again, everybody from Guardians of the
Galaxy director James Gunn to Disney CEO Bob

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.990
Iger wound up criticizing Scorsese's take
on the MCU franchise.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:27.520
Of course, it must be said that The Irishman
is an excellent film.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:33.280
Starring Scorsese mainstays like Robert De
Niro, Joe Pesci, and Harvey Keitel - and adding

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.920
no less than Al Pacino into the mix - The
Irishman has earned Scorsese some of the best

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.120
reviews of his career.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.120
It's safe to say there wouldn't be a controversy
if Scorsese had simply stuck to talking about

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:45.980
his new film while making the rounds.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.760
But that's not really his style, is it?

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:55.640
"My concern is losing the screens to massive
'theme park' films."

